# Citation metadata for CoMeta (Citation File Format 1.2.0).
# GitHub shows a "Cite this repository" button from this file, and Zenodo uses
# it when the release is archived.
# Before the release: replace the bracketed placeholders below (and the same
# names in COMETA_AUTHORS, Cell 1 of the notebook). After Zenodo assigns a DOI,
# uncomment the doi line here and set COMETA_DOI in Cell 1.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use CoMeta, please cite it as below."
title: "CoMeta: A no-code, glass-box platform for multilevel meta-analysis"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-10-24"   # set to the date of the GitHub release
# doi: 10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/ErickJLA/CoMeta"
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: "Erick"
    family-names: "[SURNAME]"
    affiliation: "[AFFILIATION]"
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
  - given-names: "Karla"
    family-names: "[SURNAME]"
    affiliation: "[AFFILIATION]"
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
  - given-names: "Hugo"
    family-names: "[SURNAME]"
    affiliation: "[AFFILIATION]"
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
abstract: >-
  CoMeta is a point-and-click Google Colab notebook for three-level
  meta-analysis in ecology: effect-size calculation with shared-control
  covariances, three-level random-effects models with cluster-robust (CR2)
  inference, subgroups, meta-regression and splines, the proportion of
  meaningful effects, publication-bias and sensitivity analyses, and
  publication-ready figures and text. All statistics are visible in the
  notebook, and a validation suite compares them with R on the user's own data.
keywords:
  - meta-analysis
  - three-level model
  - multilevel meta-analysis
  - ecology
  - cluster-robust variance estimation
  - Google Colab
